# NONE OF THIS IS REQUIRED TO START.
#
# With no VITE_SUPABASE_* set, the app runs its no-DB adapter against the
# bundled sample and serves reads — that is the first run, and it needs no
# configuration at all. Fill this in when you want a database behind it.
# See docs/connectors/backend-contract.md for what a backend has to answer.
#
# Vite exposes only variables prefixed with VITE_
# Local Supabase (after `npm run supabase:start` — see CLI output for keys):
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# Hosted Supabase project (Dashboard → Settings → API):
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local authoring key — OPTIONAL, dev server only, NEVER deployed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The deployed app is read-only to the public: write policies are `to
# authenticated`, and a browser visitor is anon. It does carry a sign-in — a
# password form and a magic link, in its settings — so an account with the
# editing tier writes from the deployed site; the magic link is sent with
# account creation off, so it cannot mint one. Set this to author slices and
# cell specs from `npm run dev` against a database you already administer.
#
# It is a service-role key: anyone who can load a page built with it has full
# database access. Therefore —
#   * put it in `.env.local` (gitignored via `.env.*`), not in this file;
#   * never set it in Netlify or any other deploy environment;
#   * the app ignores it entirely in production builds (`import.meta.env.DEV`).
# A visible "authoring" badge appears in the app while it is in use.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_DEV_SERVICE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Edit UI preview — OPTIONAL, dev server only, grants nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The authoring surfaces (View/Edit switch, Make slice, the change counter)
# are hidden when the session cannot write, which is right for a deployed
# visitor and unhelpful when you are building them. Set this to show them on
# `npm run dev` without a service key anywhere near the bundle.
#
# It is not a permission. RLS and the function grants are the authority and
# neither reads it: holding only the anon key, a write still comes back
# "permission denied for function upsert_cell". An "edit preview" badge
# appears in the app so the state is never mistaken for real authoring.
#
# VITE_DEV_AUTHORING_UI=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev sign-in — the recommended way to author locally. Dev server only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A real Supabase auth user: RLS sees `authenticated` exactly as designed,
# nothing is bypassed, and no service key goes anywhere near a browser.
# The app signs in automatically on boot when both are set. Put the real
# values in `.env.local` (gitignored), never in this file or any deploy env.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_DEV_EMAIL=
# VITE_SUPABASE_DEV_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base path — OPTIONAL, build time. Unset serves the app from the domain root.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set it to serve the app under a path, e.g. https://example.org/demo/. The
# build is written to dist/demo/, and every URL the app reads or writes keeps
# the prefix. The build writes the prefixed redirect and cache rules into
# dist/_redirects and dist/_headers, so a host's build setting is enough.
# Recipe: docs/guide/04-operations.md § Serving from a path.
#
# BASE_PATH=/demo/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scratch PostgREST checks — OPTIONAL, needs a reachable Postgres 17.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# npm run slice:cell-edit:postgrest
# npm run test:server-context:postgrest
# Both runners build the seed-load stack, mint an authenticated owner claim,
# and remove the scratch database afterwards. CI uses pinned PostgREST v16.3;
# no binary on PATH or POSTGREST_BIN means an explicit unverified result.
# Set runner options in the shell (or export them from your gitignored .env).
# POSTGREST_BIN=/path/to/postgrest
# SLICE_DB=slice_over_postgrest
# SLICE_PORT=3111
# PGHOST=localhost
# PGPORT=5432
# PGUSER=
# PGPASSWORD=
# The runners supply these two to Vitest; leave them unset for fast tests.
# SLICE_POSTGREST_URL=
# SLICE_POSTGREST_JWT=
